+# Daily Grind Rewards
+
+A responsive rewards sign-up homepage with native browser email verification. The Node server serves the page, issues per-form challenges, verifies email ownership, and saves verified rewards accounts. No OTP, magic link, or verification email is sent.
+
+## Runtime and configuration
+
+Requires Node.js 22 or later and pnpm. Install dependencies with `pnpm install`. The production command is `pnpm start` (a Node HTTP server, no compilation needed).
+
+Set environment variables through your deployment platform; `.env.example` documents the settings (the app does not automatically load `.env` files):
+
+- `APP_ORIGIN`: the exact public HTTPS origin, with no trailing slash. Required when `NODE_ENV=production`; the verification token audience and submitted Origin must match it. The local default is `http://localhost:11222`.
+- `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN`: register your public origin for Chrome's Email Verification origin trial and provide the issued token. The server includes it in the page's `Origin-Trial` response header. No token is fabricated or included in source.
+- `EVP_TRUSTED_ORIGINS`: comma-separated HTTPS origins permitted for issuer metadata and public key retrieval. Defaults to `https://accounts.google.com,https://www.googleapis.com`. To support another provider, add its issuer and JWKS origin after reviewing its metadata. Every email domain must still delegate to the issuer through its DNS TXT record.
+- `DATA_DIR`: a private, persistent directory for `accounts.json`; defaults to `./data`. Do not put it in a public static directory.
+- `PORT`: defaults to `11222`.
+
+Terminate HTTPS at a trusted reverse proxy in production. This implementation uses a bounded, in-memory session/nonce and rate-limit store and a serialized, atomic JSON account store. Run **one Node process** with a persistent volume. For horizontal scaling, replace these stores with a shared database and atomic nonce consumption. Sessions expire after ten minutes; restarting the process invalidates open forms. The rate limit uses socket IP and deliberately ignores untrusted forwarding headers; configure an appropriate trusted proxy IP strategy before placing it behind a shared reverse proxy at scale.
+
+## Verification flow
+
+1. `GET /` renders a fresh 256-bit nonce into the hidden `autocomplete="email-verification-token"` input, inside the email sign-up form. The expected nonce lives only in server-side state keyed by an HttpOnly session cookie. HTTPS cookies are Secure. Pages use `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+2. In a participating browser, entering an email address allows the browser to obtain a signed email verification token using the customer's active email-provider session. The native form submission provides this token; JavaScript does not intercept submission or claim email verification.
+3. `POST /api/verify-email` consumes the single-use challenge before any asynchronous verification. It checks the request origin, fields and rate limit; compares the email case-insensitively; checks DNS delegation; validates issuer metadata; verifies the EVT signature and expiration; and verifies the browser key-binding signature, nonce, audience, freshness and `sd_hash` with `@sd-jwt/core` and `jose`.
+4. Only after successful cryptographic verification does the endpoint create the account with `emailVerified: true` and a salted scrypt password hash. No passwords or tokens are logged. The response shows a confirmation; a duplicate account is not created.
+
+Native submissions return the homepage with an inline success or retry notice. Clients requesting `Accept: application/json` receive `{ success, message, code }` and the corresponding HTTP status instead. The endpoint expects URL-encoded form data and a session cookie from the homepage, not arbitrary JSON.
+
+Browser verification remains experimental and requires a supported Chrome version, a valid origin-trial setup, a participating email provider, and an active provider session in the same browser. **When no token is provided or verification fails, the server does not create or mark an account verified and does not send email.** It explains how to retry. An existing email verification fallback was not available in the original site, and the requested no-email behavior is preserved.
+
+This change implements account registration and initial rewards membership storage. Point accrual, account sign-in, and order integration are outside this registration flow.
+
+## Checks
+
+- `pnpm test`: in-process Request/Response tests using locally signed cryptographic fixtures and temporary account storage. No sockets are opened and the server entrypoint is never imported.
+- `pnpm build`: syntax checks only; does not start a server or import the server entrypoint.
+
+Tests cover valid verification, key rotation without `kid`, invalid signatures, email mismatch, wrong audience/nonce/hash, expired tokens, missing key binding, DNS and metadata rejection, session isolation, nonce replay/expiry, cross-origin requests, missing browser tokens, durable password hashing, duplicates, and rate limits.
+
+## References
+
+- [Chrome Email Verification API](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification)
+- [Server verification implementation guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/security/verify-email-ownership)
+- [Email Verification API specification](https://wicg.github.io/email-verification/)
+
+Photography is loaded from Unsplash; typefaces are loaded from Google Fonts. Layout, forms and policies remain usable with system font fallbacks.
